mp": "2026-04-11T16:21:21Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Where is this ?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:21:26Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Yes, but it is awe inspiring", "role": "confirmation", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:21:27Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Istanbul", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:21:28Z", "username": "Sidhesh K K", "text": "Ab gazelle skin itna tika wohi badi baat hai", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:21:37Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "What people were able to achieve", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:22:23Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Next", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:22:27Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "In 1973, a German historian and documentary filmmaker stood before a press conference in Bonn and unveiled what he called the first truly equitable representation of the Earth. His argument was political as much as cartographic: the map that hung in classrooms across the Western world — designed by a Flemish cartographer in 1569 to assist sailors in plotting straight-line compass courses — inflated areas near the poles drastically. The result made Europe and North America appear far larger than they actually were, while equatorial regions — where most of the world's developing nations lay — were correspondingly diminished.\nHis alternative preserved equal area: every region occupied exactly the space proportional to its true surface on Earth. Africa, at over eleven times the land area of Greenland, appeared as such. But shapes were badly distorted. The cartographic establishment was furious. Critics noted that the historian had essentially republished a projection already described by a Scottish clergyman and amateur astronomer in 1855 — and was claiming it as his own invention.\nThe map was adopted by development organisations, the World Council of Churches, and the United Nations. It appeared as a plot device in The West Wing. A projection had become a culture war.\nWhat is this projection called, and who was the German historian who controversially popularised it in 1973 — drawing on work by a 19th-century Scottish clergyman?", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:22:49Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Mercator", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:22:52Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Mercator", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:22:55Z", "username": "Akshay", "text": "Gall peters", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:23:03Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "peters", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:23:06Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Peters", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:23:11Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "It is gall peters", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:23:12Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "peters projection", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:23:25Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Named after James gall", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T1